<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Pure layout conversion of BeamV0.mlapp's startup screen - the state
  visible immediately after launch, before any user click: the default-
  selected "Sonicate" tab, plus every control parented directly to
  app.UIFigure (the 3 MRI axes, their sliders/step-buttons/mm labels,
  the CRF fields, the overlay checkboxes, System Status, Brightness
  up/down - all visible on every tab, not just this one).

  Source: BeamV0/GUIMatlab/BEAM/GUI/BeamV0.mlapp, matlab/document.xml's
  createComponents method. Every geometry below is a direct, mechanical
  conversion of that file's own app.<Name>.Position = [x y w h]:
    - MATLAB's uifigure coordinate origin is bottom-left, Y increasing
      upward; Qt's is top-left, Y increasing downward. Every position
      here is qt_y = parent_content_height - matlab_y - height, done by
      hand against each real parent's own height (789 for app.UIFigure
      itself; a uitab's own content height is its uitabgroup's height
      minus that group's tab-strip, ~22px, since App Designer doesn't
      store the strip height as its own property).
    - No ValueChangedFcn/ButtonPushedFcn/etc. carried over - this file
      is layout only, deliberately, so it can be reviewed as a design
      before any logic is wired to it.
    - uiaxes (the 3 MRI views, the Register/Sonicate tab's own plot
      axes) have no Qt Designer equivalent - stood in here as a plain
      framed QLabel showing just the axes' own title text, matching
      their real Position exactly. The real charts (QtCharts, this
      port's own MriSliceView, etc.) replace these placeholders later;
      this file only fixes where the *box* goes.
    - uilamp (ConnectedLamp) has no Designer equivalent either - stood
      in as a small circular QLabel via styleSheet border-radius.
    - Several controls are genuinely hidden at real startup
      (Visible='off' in the source, before any click makes them
      relevant): TriggerSwitch/its label, the Overlay Transparency
      slider/label, Overlay Brain Volume button, Show Overlay Brain
      checkbox. Included here (matching their real, mostly off-figure
      Position) but with visible="false", not omitted - this screen is
      what's *there*, not just what's drawn.
    - Object names match the source's own app.<Name> exactly, so wiring
      this up later can find each one by the same name already used
      throughout the rest of this port.
-->
